mal pH for activity. 胃蛋白酶合剂 loses efficacy above pH 5 and should not be combined with alkaline agents [Data: Relationships (48957)]. Similarly, 胰酶 is indicated for消化不良 (indigestion) [Data: Relationships (24238)] but may be compromised by co-administration with agents that alter gastrointestinal pH or bind proteins. Moreover, 胃蛋白酶合剂 interacts negatively with 煅龙骨, an alkaline traditional Chinese medicine, due to acid-base neutralization that diminishes therapeutic effect [Data: Relationships (43478)]. These findings underscore the need for precise formulation and dosing guidance to preserve enzymatic function.\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"美加明 participates in clinically significant pharmacodynamic interactions\",\n            \"explanation\": \"美加明 (Mecamylamine), though less frequently referenced (degree = 2), is involved in two notable interactions. It is contraindicated with磺脲类药物 (sulfonylurea antidiabetics), as co-administration can lead to dangerous drug-drug interactions classified as 'X-level' (highest severity) in clinical databases [Data: Relationships (47175)]. Additionally, it interacts with 稀盐酸 (dilute hydrochloric acid), though the exact mechanism is not detailed beyond general discussion of gastrointestinal absorption interference [Data: Relationships (47304)]. Given its role as an older antihypertensive agent with narrow therapeutic utility today, its presence in this network may reflect legacy formulary data, but the sulfonylurea interaction remains a serious clinical concern if co-prescribed.\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Traditional Chinese medicine 煅龙骨 interacts with acidic Western drugs\",\n            \"explanation\": \"煅龙骨 (Calcined Dragon Bone), a traditional Chinese medicinal substance with alkaline properties, is documented to reduce the efficacy of acidic Western drugs like 胃蛋白酶合剂 through acid-base neutralization [Data: Relationships (43478)]. This exemplifies a broader category of incompatibiliti